WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.042
This query first creates a wide product-level table with month totals.

00:00:04.042 --> 00:00:07.737
Then it unpivots the wide columns into product-month rows.

00:00:07.737 --> 00:00:12.072
The result is long again, but it is not the original transaction table.

00:00:12.072 --> 00:00:14.697
It is one row per product-month total.

00:00:14.697 --> 00:00:18.897
That is the key distinction between reshaping and recovering detail.

00:00:18.897 --> 00:00:23.622
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:23.622 --> 00:00:27.722
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:27.722 --> 00:00:31.597
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:31.597 --> 00:00:37.012
In a real setting such as a machine-learning workflow converting wide measurements into tidy long data,

00:00:37.012 --> 00:00:42.782
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:42.782 --> 00:00:44.227
before discussing syntax.

00:00:44.227 --> 00:00:49.920
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
